course: history
lesson: 79
title: Worries and hopes: climate, the UN, space, AI, and is the world getting better?
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Ask a class «is the world getting better?» and it splits in half, and both halves can bring real evidence: fewer children die, more go to school and fewer people are very poor than ever before, yet the planet is warming, many wild animal numbers have fallen, and wars, including Russia's invasion of Ukraine, still destroy lives.
explain: The researcher Max Roser put it in three sentences that are all true at once: the world is much better, the world is awful, and the world can be much better.
explain: News makes the picture look darker than it is, because a sudden disaster makes a headline while a slow good change, like a vaccine saving lives year after year, almost never does; Gran sighing «it's all bad news» is seeing the news, not the whole world.
explain: There are real stories of fixing things together: countries agreed in 1987 to ban the chemicals eating the ozone layer, and it is slowly healing; solar power became about ten times cheaper in ten years; vaccines for COVID-19 were ready within a year.
explain: Unlike Europa Universalis, which stops in 1821, the real game has no end date and no single player: governments, scientists and millions of ordinary people, kids included, all move the pieces together.
fact: The Earth is about 1.2 to 1.3 °C warmer than in the late 1800s, mainly from burning coal, oil and gas; 2024 was the first calendar year reported above 1.5 °C warmer.
fact: Scientists agree that humans are causing the warming; people still argue about how fast to change and who should pay.
fact: In 1987 countries agreed in the Montreal Protocol to ban chemicals that damaged the ozone layer, and the ozone layer is slowly healing.
fact: Solar power became about ten times cheaper between 2010 and 2020: what cost ten pounds in 2010 cost about one pound in 2020.
fact: The United Nations was founded in 1945 with 51 countries and now has 193; in its Security Council five countries can each block any decision alone, called a veto, which many people criticise, and it often cannot stop wars.
fact: Some researchers say deaths in wars between countries fell after 1945; others point to recent years with more fighting, like Russia's war on Ukraine since 2014 and its full-scale invasion in 2022, so whether the world is more peaceful is contested.
fact: More than 600 people have been to space; 12 walked on the Moon between 1969 and 1972, and the International Space Station has had people on board since 2000.
fact: AI helped scientists predict the shapes of proteins, the tiny building parts of every living thing, work that won a Nobel Prize in 2024; people also worry about jobs, fake videos and safety.
fact: COVID-19 vaccines were made within a year in 2020, faster than ever before, by using many years of earlier research.
fact: Many wild animal populations have fallen since 1970; experts argue about exactly how much.
fact: Health, schooling and poverty have improved a lot; climate and nature are worse or at risk; Max Roser says the world is much better, awful, and can be much better, all at once.
fact: News reports sudden bad events, while slow good changes rarely make headlines, so the world can seem worse than the data shows.
fact: Kids can learn, share ideas and make small changes, but big problems need many people and governments, so children should not carry them alone.
exercise: About how much warmer is the Earth than in the late 1800s? | About 1.2 to 1.3 °C | About 12 °C | About 0.01 °C | Not warmer at all
exercise: What is the main cause of the warming? | Burning coal, oil and gas | The Sun moving closer every year | Volcanoes alone | Cows breathing out heat
exercise: Which year was the first reported above 1.5 °C warmer? | 2024 | 1900 | 1987 | 2000
exercise: What did countries agree in 1987? | To ban chemicals that damaged the ozone layer | To stop all cars and lorries driving in every city | To send people to live on Mars before the year 2000 | To ban solar panels because they damaged the sky
exercise: Why are there so many solar panels now? | Solar power became about ten times cheaper in ten years | Governments banned all other kinds of power, including coal and gas | Panels started growing on their own | The Sun got brighter
exercise: When was the United Nations founded? | In 1945 | In 1066 | In 1999 | In 1815
exercise: How many countries are in the UN now? | 193 | 51 | 12 | 500
exercise: Why do many people criticise the UN Security Council? | Five countries can each block its decisions | It has no members | It meets only once a century | It is run by one king
exercise: Are there fewer wars now than in the past? | It is contested: some measures say yes, recent years say otherwise | Yes, war has ended everywhere | No, there were never wars before | Nobody has ever studied war
exercise: How many people have walked on the Moon? | 12 | 600 | 1 | None
exercise: What did AI help scientists do, winning a Nobel Prize in 2024? | Predict the shapes of proteins | Walk on Mars | Find the lost city of Atlantis | Stop all earthquakes
exercise: How were COVID-19 vaccines made so fast? | They used many years of earlier research | They skipped all the testing to save a lot of time | They were found ready-made in a cave in the jungle | They were made back in 1900 and kept in a freezer
exercise: Have many wild animal populations fallen since 1970? | Yes, though experts argue about how much | No, every one of them has doubled since then | Wild animals have never been counted by anyone | Only the numbers of pets have fallen, not wild ones
exercise: Which three sentences did Max Roser say are all true? | The world is much better, it is awful, and it can be much better | The world is perfect, finished and boring | The world is getting worse in every way | Nothing ever changes, nothing really matters, and nothing ever helps
exercise: Why can the news make the world seem worse than it is? | Sudden bad events make headlines; slow good changes rarely do | Journalists are never allowed by law to report any good things at all | Good things never happen | The news is always invented
exercise: Can kids make a difference? | Yes, in small ways, but big problems need many people and governments | No, never in any way | Yes, kids must solve everything alone | Only kids who are famous
test: Ruby's uncle: "Global warming's a hoax, mate, it snowed last week." Best answer? | One cold week is weather; the long-term climate is about 1.3 °C warmer | He's right, mate: a week of snow proves the planet is not warming at all | The Earth is getting colder every year | Nobody has ever measured temperatures
test: Max: "The ozone hole thing, did they fix it or nah?" What's the honest answer? | Countries banned the harmful chemicals in 1987 and it is slowly healing | It was fixed in one day in 1950 | Nothing was ever done | Nah, it just got bigger and bigger every year until it swallowed the Moon
test: Ella's Gen X dad: "Solar panels? Total bummer, way too pricey." ("Bummer" means a letdown.) What changed? | Solar power got about ten times cheaper between 2010 and 2020 | Solar panels cost about ten times more now than they did back in 2010 | Solar panels stopped working | They are only used on the Moon
test: Josh: "The UN runs the whole world, right? Like a final boss?" ("Final boss" is the strongest enemy at the end of a game.) What's true? | It helps countries talk, but has no power to rule them and often cannot stop wars | It rules every country like a king | It has no members at all | It was closed down in 1950
test: Danylo: "Are there fewer wars now?" His dad: "It's complicated, synku." ("Synku" means son, in Ukrainian.) Why is it complicated? | Some measures show fewer war deaths after 1945, but recent years saw more fighting | All wars ended in 1945 | There were never any wars before 1945 | Nobody knows what a war is
test: Leo's mate: "Loads of people go to space now, my cousin's going next week, no cap." What's more exact? | Only a bit over 600 people have ever been to space | Millions of people go up every single year now, no cap | Nobody has ever been, it was all filmed in a studio | Everyone over 18 has been once, it's like a school trip
test: Aisha says AI helps doctors. Her mate: "AI's gonna take over, bruv, it's giving Terminator." ("It's giving" means it looks like.) Fairest view? | Both the hopes and the worries are real, and people are working on safety | AI has never helped anyone | AI already rules the world | Worrying about AI is always silly, because computers can never ever go wrong
test: Mila's neighbour: "Them vaccines were rushed, done in a weekend." What's true? | They were made within a year using many years of earlier research | They were made in a weekend with no research at all, just guessing | They were invented back in 1900 and just kept on a shelf until 2020 | No vaccine was ever really made; people all just got better on their own
test: Oliver: "Are animals disappearing? Like, all of them?" Best answer? | Many wild populations have fallen since 1970, though experts argue how much | All animals have vanished | Animals are doing better than ever everywhere, with more of every kind since 1970 | Only dinosaurs have disappeared
test: The teacher asks, "Is the world getting better?" Half the class yells "Yes!", half "Nah, it's cooked!" Who's right? | Both sides have real evidence: health and poverty improved, climate and nature worsened | Only the "yes" side | Only the "no" side | Nobody, because the world never changes
test: Gran watches the telly: "It's all bad news, love." What would happen if the news reported slow good changes as often as disasters? | The world would seem less hopeless, closer to what the data shows | The world would get worse | Nothing would be different | People would stop watching all news, because good news is always boring
test: Kai: "Can kids make a difference, or is that just a vibe?" What's the wise answer? | Yes, in small ways, but big problems need many people and governments | No, kids can never change anything | Yes, and kids must fix the climate all alone, without any help from adults | Only by buying more things
exam: Identify the principal cause of the warming of the Earth observed since the late nineteenth century. | The combustion of coal, oil and gas by human beings | The approach of the Sun towards the Earth year by year | The activity of volcanoes alone, without any human part | The respiration of cattle and other domestic animals
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the outcome of the agreement concluded in nineteen hundred and eighty-seven in respect of the ozone layer? | The harmful chemicals were prohibited and the ozone layer is gradually recovering | No measures were ever adopted | The ozone layer was wholly destroyed | The agreement prohibited the use of solar energy
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the United Nations. | It was founded in nineteen hundred and forty-five and now comprises one hundred and ninety-three member states | It exercises sovereign rule over every state of the world | It was founded in eighteen hundred and fifteen with five members | It was dissolved in nineteen hundred and fifty
exam: Ascertain the response most consistent with the evidence in respect of the question whether the world is improving. | Health, schooling and poverty have improved, whereas climate and nature have worsened | Every aspect of human life has deteriorated, without any exception, since nineteen hundred | Every aspect of human life is now without defect | No change of any kind has occurred since nineteen hundred
